From 56499f34819629d2070ce5fed324d6d864d58827 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Berkay=20K=C3=B6sebay?= <brkosebay@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 18:27:02 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] power: supply: apple_tristar_charger: Suspend without waiting
 for the source

Suspend returned -EBUSY while a source was being identified or its grant
was ramping, so that the identification could finish first. It never can:
the poll work runs on a freezable workqueue, which is frozen before device
suspend, so the phase stays where it was and every try of the sleep fails
the same way. On an iPad 5 with a Mac cable, three rtcwake -m freeze in a
row each ended with "platform_pm_suspend returns -16" from this driver.

iOS 16 (AppleARMPMUCharger, sleep handler) cancels the ramp on sleep and
leaves the input limit as written; after wake the ramp goes on from there.
Do the same. A ramping grant is kept like a finished one, under the same
conditions (a plug wake event, no USB host), and continues on resume: the
limit it reached is never above the grant. An identification in progress
is abandoned and parks at the handoff, which the source draws already;
it runs within one poll, so the mux is restored by the time suspend can
take the lock. The source is identified again on resume.

Assisted-by: LLM
---
 drivers/power/supply/apple_tristar_charger.c | 19 ++++++++++++-------
 1 file changed, 12 insertions(+), 7 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/power/supply/apple_tristar_charger.c b/drivers/power/supply/apple_tristar_charger.c
index e81ffae..608c6fd 100644
--- a/drivers/power/supply/apple_tristar_charger.c
+++ b/drivers/power/supply/apple_tristar_charger.c
@@ -1593,7 +1593,16 @@ static void tristar_shutdown(struct platform_device *pdev)
  * identified charger keeps its limit: a tablet left on one charges at full
  * rate. A USB host may be asleep too and is owed no more than the handoff, so
  * it parks there and is identified again on resume, as is anything without a
- * plug event to rely on. An identification in progress finishes first.
+ * plug event to rely on.
+ *
+ * Sleep never waits for the charger. iOS 16 (AppleARMPMUCharger) cancels the
+ * ramp on sleep and leaves the limit as written; after wake the ramp goes on
+ * from there. So does this driver: a ramp in progress keeps the limit it has
+ * reached, which is never above the grant, and continues on resume. An
+ * identification in progress is abandoned: it runs within one poll, so the
+ * mux is back in place here, and the source draws the handoff it already
+ * has until it is identified again on resume. The poll work is frozen by
+ * now, so waiting for either would wait forever.
  */
 static int tristar_suspend(struct device *dev)
 {
@@ -1602,13 +1611,9 @@ static int tristar_suspend(struct device *dev)
 	int ret = 0;
 
 	mutex_lock(&chg->lock);
-	if (chg->present && (chg->phase == TRISTAR_SETTLING ||
-			     chg->phase == TRISTAR_RAMPING)) {
-		mutex_unlock(&chg->lock);
-		return -EBUSY;
-	}
 	keep = chg->plug_irq && device_may_wakeup(dev) && chg->present &&
-	       chg->phase == TRISTAR_CHARGING && !tristar_usb_ma(chg);
+	       (chg->phase == TRISTAR_RAMPING || chg->phase == TRISTAR_CHARGING) &&
+	       !tristar_usb_ma(chg);
 	chg->stopping = true;
 	if (!keep) {
 		chg->present = false;
